CHECK_EQ(taskObservers_.load(), nullptr) requires glog to provide
google::MakeCheckOpValueString<std::nullptr_t>(), a non-template
overload that must be defined and exported from the linked libglog
binary rather than instantiated inline. glog only needs this overload
in the first place because `ostream << nullptr` is ambiguous under
libc++ (candidates: bool and const void*); under libstdc++ the same
expression resolves cleanly via the generic template with no external
symbol required.

On at least one current libc++/glog pairing (macOS arm64, both the
distro-packaged glog and a from-source rebuild) that overload is
declared in glog/logging.h but not actually exported from the built
libglog, producing:

  Undefined symbols for architecture arm64:
    "void google::MakeCheckOpValueString<std::nullptr_t>(...)",
    referenced from ... in ThreadPoolExecutor.cpp.o

See: https://github.com/facebook/folly/issues/2681
     https://github.com/google/glog/issues/756

Rewriting the check as a boolean CHECK() avoids the whole
MakeCheckOpValueString<nullptr_t> path -- no per-operand
stringification is needed, so no ambiguity and no dependency on the
symbol being present. Behavior is unchanged (still fatal on
mismatch); only the failure message's operand-value detail is
dropped, which added nothing here since one side is always nullptr.
---
 folly/executors/ThreadPoolExecutor.cpp | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/folly/executors/ThreadPoolExecutor.cpp b/folly/executors/ThreadPoolExecutor.cpp
index c81b5e1..a0319e6 100644
--- a/folly/executors/ThreadPoolExecutor.cpp
+++ b/folly/executors/ThreadPoolExecutor.cpp
@@ -68,7 +68,7 @@ ThreadPoolExecutor::~ThreadPoolExecutor() {
   // Derived class destructors must call destroyTaskObservers() after stop().
   // This ensures observer cleanup (e.g., fb303 callback unregistration) runs
   // while the derived vtable is still valid, preventing pure virtual calls.
-  CHECK_EQ(taskObservers_.load(), nullptr)
+  CHECK(taskObservers_.load() == nullptr)
       << "ThreadPoolExecutor subclasses should call destroyTaskObservers() in their destructor";
 }
